          "guid": "mszhzdolvfpkb14h",
          "message": {
            "text": "Observing node.getRootNode() with subtree:true (not the node's parent) is what makes ANCESTOR removal fire teardown, not just direct removal.\n\nThe teardown must run whenever the node leaves the document — a morph replacing it, remountOn clearing its parent, or a grandparent being unmounted. A childList observer on the node's direct parent would miss the last case: removing an ancestor higher up mutates that ancestor's child list, not the node's parent's, yet node.isConnected still flips to false. Observing the root with subtree:true means every structural mutation anywhere in the tree re-checks isConnected, so all three removal shapes converge on one check. The test \"runs teardown when an ANCESTOR is removed (subtree observation)\" pins the case a parent-only observer would silently drop; \"a mutation elsewhere while the node stays connected does NOT tear down\" pins the other side (a fired observer with the node still attached is a no-op).\n\nCost note: this is one document-wide observer per imperative() call. Fine for the handful of library-owned widgets a page has; if a page created hundreds, a single shared observer keyed by a WeakSet would be the optimization — deliberately not done here (KISS for v1).\n",
            "markdown": "Observing `node.getRootNode()` with `subtree:true` (not the node's parent) is what makes ANCESTOR removal fire teardown, not just direct removal.\n\nThe teardown must run whenever the node leaves the document — a morph replacing it, `remountOn` clearing its parent, or a grandparent being unmounted. A `childList` observer on the node's direct parent would miss the last case: removing an ancestor higher up mutates *that* ancestor's child list, not the node's parent's, yet `node.isConnected` still flips to false. Observing the root with `subtree:true` means every structural mutation anywhere in the tree re-checks `isConnected`, so all three removal shapes converge on one check. The test \"runs teardown when an ANCESTOR is removed (subtree observation)\" pins the case a parent-only observer would silently drop; \"a mutation elsewhere while the node stays connected does NOT tear down\" pins the other side (a fired observer with the node still attached is a no-op).\n\nCost note: this is one document-wide observer per `imperative()` call. Fine for the handful of library-owned widgets a page has; if a page created hundreds, a single shared observer keyed by a WeakSet would be the optimization — deliberately not done here (KISS for v1).\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/imperative.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 45,
                  "endLine": 53,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "  // Observe the node's live tree (the document when connected) with subtree, so\n  // an ANCESTOR removal — not just a direct one — is caught. Each mutation just\n  // re-checks `node.isConnected`, which is true until the node (or an ancestor)\n  // is removed, so a morph swap / remountOn replacement / manual removal all fire.\n  const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {\n    if (!node.isConnected) finish();\n  });\n  observer.observe(node.getRootNode(), { childList: true, subtree: true });\n"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
